1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,168
Here's a question that sounds easy.

2
00:00:02,488 --> 00:00:05,131
How many different games
of tic-tac-toe are there?

3
00:00:05,367 --> 00:00:11,168
By a game,
we mean the whole list of moves, in order.

4
00:00:11,488 --> 00:00:13,968
So if two games end with the same board,

5
00:00:13,968 --> 00:00:16,730
but the moves happened
in a different order,

6
00:00:16,730 --> 00:00:18,865
they count as two different games.

7
00:00:19,185 --> 00:00:23,028
Flipped or turned versions
count separately too.

8
00:00:24,067 --> 00:00:25,814
Take a guess. A hundred?

9
00:00:26,134 --> 00:00:30,017
A million?
Pause the video and write your guess down.

10
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:41,000
The answer is 255,168.

11
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:44,881
In this video we'll find out
where that number comes from:

12
00:00:44,881 --> 00:00:46,478
first by careful counting,

13
00:00:46,478 --> 00:00:50,604
and then with a short computer
program that plays every single game.

14
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:53,552
Let's start with a simpler question.

15
00:00:53,872 --> 00:00:56,445
Suppose the players ignore three in a row,

16
00:00:56,445 --> 00:00:59,630
and just keep going until
all nine squares are full.

17
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:02,748
In how many different orders
can the board fill up?

18
00:01:02,983 --> 00:01:05,559
X goes first and has 9
squares to choose from.

19
00:01:05,879 --> 00:01:09,047
For each of those, O has 8 squares left.

20
00:01:09,367 --> 00:01:12,669
That's 9 groups of 8,
so the choices multiply:

21
00:01:12,669 --> 00:01:16,905
9 times 8 is 72 different ways
to make the first two moves.

22
00:01:17,150 --> 00:01:21,953
Keep going: 9 times 8 times 7,
and so on, all the way down to 1.

23
00:01:22,273 --> 00:01:24,869
Mathematicians have a short name for this:

24
00:01:24,869 --> 00:01:28,330
nine factorial,
written as a 9 with an exclamation mark.

25
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:31,269
It equals 362,880.

26
00:01:31,517 --> 00:01:33,097
But wait.

27
00:01:33,147 --> 00:01:36,115
That's more than 255,168.

28
00:01:36,435 --> 00:01:38,058
Our count is too high.

29
00:01:38,378 --> 00:01:41,112
Where are all those
extra games coming from?

30
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:48,516
Here's the catch. Real tic-tac-toe stops
as soon as someone gets three in a row.

31
00:01:48,836 --> 00:01:54,542
In this game, X wins on the fifth move,
so the last four squares are never played.

32
00:01:54,783 --> 00:01:59,179
But our 362,880 counted
those missing moves anyway,

33
00:01:59,179 --> 00:02:02,886
as if the players kept going after the win.

34
00:02:03,206 --> 00:02:08,980
The 4 empty squares can be filled
in 4 times 3 times 2 times 1, so 24 ways.

35
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:11,746
So this one game got counted 24 times!

36
00:02:12,066 --> 00:02:15,253
Let's call those made-up
endings ghost games.

37
00:02:15,573 --> 00:02:18,664
We want to count each real game only once.

38
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:20,930
So when can a game end?

39
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:22,850
Not before move 5.

40
00:02:23,005 --> 00:02:24,950
X plays moves 1, 3 and 5,

41
00:02:24,950 --> 00:02:29,929
so the fifth move is the first
time anyone can have three marks.

42
00:02:30,167 --> 00:02:34,906
Pause and ponder: how many games
end with X winning on move 5?

43
00:02:35,226 --> 00:02:37,488
Try to count them before I show you.

44
00:02:57,733 --> 00:02:59,908
Here's one way, in three steps.

45
00:03:00,228 --> 00:03:03,091
First, pick the line X wins with.

46
00:03:03,411 --> 00:03:07,273
There are 8: 3 rows,
3 columns and 2 diagonals.

47
00:03:07,593 --> 00:03:11,228
Second, X fills that line in some order:

48
00:03:11,228 --> 00:03:16,044
3 times 2 times 1,
which is 3 factorial, so 6 orders.

49
00:03:16,364 --> 00:03:20,363
Third, O's first mark can go
on any of the other 6 squares,

50
00:03:20,363 --> 00:03:24,633
and O's second mark on any of the 5 left:
6 times 5 is 30 ways.

51
00:03:24,953 --> 00:03:29,745
Multiply:
8 times 6 times 30 is 1,440 games.

52
00:03:31,583 --> 00:03:33,746
What about games that O wins on move 6?

53
00:03:34,066 --> 00:03:35,871
O needs three in a row.

54
00:03:36,191 --> 00:03:39,853
But careful:
if X's three marks also make a line,

55
00:03:39,853 --> 00:03:44,935
X already won on move 5,
and the game stopped before O could finish.

56
00:03:45,183 --> 00:03:50,247
Use the same three steps:
8 lines for O, 6 orders for O's marks,

57
00:03:50,247 --> 00:03:54,360
and 6 times 5 times 4 ways
to place X's three marks.

58
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:58,880
That's 5,760 ways for O to finish a line.

59
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:04,768
Then we subtract the 432 where
X's three marks made a line too,

60
00:04:04,768 --> 00:04:10,159
a row or column parallel to O's,
so X sneaked in a win first.

61
00:04:10,479 --> 00:04:13,444
That leaves 5,328 games.

62
00:04:13,683 --> 00:04:19,641
For games that end on moves 7, 8 and 9,
it gets much worse: we'd have to check

63
00:04:19,641 --> 00:04:25,141
every earlier move for a win,
and a full board might be a win or a draw.

64
00:04:25,461 --> 00:04:27,175
It turns into a tangled mess.

65
00:04:27,416 --> 00:04:30,833
When counting gets this messy,
there's another plan.

66
00:04:31,153 --> 00:04:32,730
Don't count cleverly.

67
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:35,276
Instead, teach a computer the rules,

68
00:04:35,276 --> 00:04:38,604
and let it play every possible game,
one by one.

69
00:04:39,650 --> 00:04:42,042
First, the computer needs a board.

70
00:04:42,362 --> 00:04:44,816
We'll use a list of 9 squares.

71
00:04:45,136 --> 00:04:48,162
In Python,
the programming language we're using,

72
00:04:48,162 --> 00:04:51,477
the spots in a list
are numbered starting from zero,

73
00:04:51,477 --> 00:04:53,709
so our squares are numbered 0 to 8.

74
00:04:54,029 --> 00:04:55,629
An empty square holds a dot.

75
00:04:57,017 --> 00:04:59,948
Next, the computer needs
to know what counts as a win.

76
00:05:00,268 --> 00:05:06,762
We simply list all 8 winning lines,
as groups of three square numbers.

77
00:05:07,082 --> 00:05:10,599
Squares 0, 1 and 2 make the top row.

78
00:05:10,919 --> 00:05:14,375
Squares 0, 4 and 8 make a diagonal.

79
00:05:15,317 --> 00:05:20,499
Next comes a function:
a little mini-program with a name.

80
00:05:20,819 --> 00:05:25,635
This one, called winner, checks every line.

81
00:05:25,955 --> 00:05:29,112
If all three squares
on a line hold the same mark,

82
00:05:29,112 --> 00:05:32,080
and that mark isn't a dot,
that player has won.

83
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:38,756
If no line matches, winner finds nothing,
and hands back None, which means nobody.

84
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:45,826
Now for the clever part:
a function called explore.

85
00:05:46,146 --> 00:05:47,831
We tell it whose turn it is,

86
00:05:47,831 --> 00:05:52,645
and its job is to count every game that can
still happen from the current board.

87
00:05:52,883 --> 00:05:55,247
It starts with two stopping rules.

88
00:05:55,567 --> 00:05:59,104
If someone has already won,
that's one finished game,

89
00:05:59,104 --> 00:06:01,440
so explore hands back the number 1.

90
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:06,517
If the board is full with no winner,
that's a draw, also one finished game.

91
00:06:06,767 --> 00:06:08,963
Otherwise, the game isn't over yet.

92
00:06:09,283 --> 00:06:12,185
So explore tries every empty square.

93
00:06:12,505 --> 00:06:16,842
It puts the player's mark there,
and then asks itself the same question,

94
00:06:16,842 --> 00:06:20,577
now for the other player:
how many games can happen from here?

95
00:06:20,897 --> 00:06:22,638
It adds up all the answers.

96
00:06:22,933 --> 00:06:26,472
Let's zoom into the first of those,
with O to move.

97
00:06:26,792 --> 00:06:30,733
If O takes the top-right square,
O wins: that's 1 game.

98
00:06:31,053 --> 00:06:33,965
Erase it,
and try the top-middle square instead.

99
00:06:34,285 --> 00:06:39,026
Then X fills the last square,
and it's a draw: 1 more.

100
00:06:39,346 --> 00:06:42,084
Undo both moves, and add up: 2 games.

101
00:06:42,404 --> 00:06:46,119
With the other two branches,
2 plus 1 plus 2 makes 5.

102
00:06:46,367 --> 00:06:48,166
When a function calls itself,

103
00:06:48,166 --> 00:06:52,199
asking itself the same question like this,
it's called recursive.

104
00:06:52,519 --> 00:06:55,011
That might sound like it goes on forever,

105
00:06:55,011 --> 00:06:59,204
but each call adds one more mark,
so a stopping rule always kicks in.

106
00:06:59,524 --> 00:07:01,579
Picture an upside-down tree.

107
00:07:01,899 --> 00:07:06,378
The empty board is at the top,
every possible move is a branch,

108
00:07:06,378 --> 00:07:08,796
and every finished game is a leaf.

109
00:07:09,116 --> 00:07:13,236
Explore walks down every branch,
one at a time,

110
00:07:13,236 --> 00:07:16,129
and counts the leaves it reaches.

111
00:07:16,367 --> 00:07:18,980
There's one more line,
and it's easy to miss.

112
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:23,460
The program plays every game on just
one board, like a single whiteboard.

113
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:27,446
So after exploring a move,
it erases that mark.

114
00:07:27,766 --> 00:07:32,756
That's called undoing the move,
and it means the next branch starts fresh.

115
00:07:33,076 --> 00:07:37,028
Trying a path, then stepping back to try
the next one, is called backtracking.

116
00:07:39,050 --> 00:07:42,812
A great way to understand
a program is to break it on purpose.

117
00:07:43,132 --> 00:07:47,621
Pause and ponder: what do you think
happens if we delete the undo line?

118
00:07:57,867 --> 00:07:59,467
The program prints just 3.

119
00:07:59,674 --> 00:08:02,311
Without undo, nothing ever gets erased.

120
00:08:02,631 --> 00:08:06,985
The program plays one game,
filling the squares in order,

121
00:08:06,985 --> 00:08:09,659
until X makes a diagonal on move 7.

122
00:08:09,979 --> 00:08:11,287
That's 1.

123
00:08:11,337 --> 00:08:16,219
Then it tries X's other choices,
but the old X is still there.

124
00:08:16,539 --> 00:08:19,047
X lands on the bottom-middle square,

125
00:08:19,047 --> 00:08:22,739
and the old diagonal counts as a win again:
that's 2.

126
00:08:23,059 --> 00:08:25,749
Then the bottom-right square: that's 3.

127
00:08:26,069 --> 00:08:29,075
Now the board is full,
so there's nothing left to try.

128
00:08:29,317 --> 00:08:33,323
Now put undo back,
and delete the winner check instead,

129
00:08:33,323 --> 00:08:36,383
so games only stop when the board is full.

130
00:08:36,703 --> 00:08:38,753
Pause and predict the answer.

131
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,197
362,880.

132
00:08:51,517 --> 00:08:53,117
That's nine factorial!

133
00:08:53,170 --> 00:08:58,133
Without the winner check,
the program keeps playing after a win,

134
00:08:58,133 --> 00:09:00,692
so it counts all the ghost games.

135
00:09:01,012 --> 00:09:03,957
It rediscovered our very first count.

136
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:09,089
Now let's put every line back
and run the real program.

137
00:09:09,409 --> 00:09:13,308
After a moment, it prints 255,168.

138
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:17,329
Let's split that total
by when each game ends.

139
00:09:17,649 --> 00:09:25,259
1,440 end on move 5, and 5,328 on move 6,
the same numbers we found by hand.

140
00:09:25,579 --> 00:09:32,616
Then the bars shoot up: about 48 thousand
end on move 7, and 73 thousand on move 8.

141
00:09:32,936 --> 00:09:37,008
Just over half of all
games use all 9 moves:

142
00:09:37,008 --> 00:09:41,080
about 82 thousand X wins,
plus 46,080 draws.

143
00:09:41,401 --> 00:09:44,345
Together, that's 255,168.

144
00:09:44,583 --> 00:09:47,670
And here's how it connects
to nine factorial.

145
00:09:47,990 --> 00:09:53,437
Remember, nine factorial counted each game
that ends on move 5 a total of 24 times,

146
00:09:53,437 --> 00:09:55,209
once for each ghost ending.

147
00:09:55,529 --> 00:09:59,065
A game ending on move
6 was counted 6 times,

148
00:09:59,065 --> 00:10:00,752
and on move 7, twice.

149
00:10:01,072 --> 00:10:05,382
Games that end on move 8 or 9 have
at most one empty square,

150
00:10:05,382 --> 00:10:07,609
so they were counted just once.

151
00:10:07,929 --> 00:10:11,044
Multiply each bar by its number,
add them up,

152
00:10:11,044 --> 00:10:13,882
and you get exactly nine factorial again.

153
00:10:16,783 --> 00:10:18,383
Who wins the most games?

154
00:10:18,545 --> 00:10:27,133
X wins 131,184 of them,
O wins 77,904, and 46,080 are draws.

155
00:10:27,453 --> 00:10:30,565
X wins in more than half
of all possible games,

156
00:10:30,565 --> 00:10:33,743
because going first gives
X more chances to win.

157
00:10:33,983 --> 00:10:35,355
One last puzzle.

158
00:10:35,405 --> 00:10:39,279
Which first move for X leads
to the most different games:

159
00:10:39,279 --> 00:10:41,522
a corner, an edge, or the center?

160
00:10:41,842 --> 00:10:43,577
Pause and guess.

161
00:10:53,817 --> 00:11:01,362
Surprise: the edge, with 29,592 games.

162
00:11:01,682 --> 00:11:08,220
Each corner gives 27,732,
and the center the fewest, just 25,872.

163
00:11:08,540 --> 00:11:14,975
Why? The center sits on 4 winning lines,
a corner on 3, and an edge on just 2.

164
00:11:15,295 --> 00:11:19,940
More lines give X more chances
to win early, on move 5 or 7,

165
00:11:19,940 --> 00:11:24,120
and every early win chops
whole branches off the tree.

166
00:11:24,367 --> 00:11:28,213
But careful:
more games doesn't mean a better move.

167
00:11:28,533 --> 00:11:33,258
Out of all the games that start
in the center, X wins about 6 in 10.

168
00:11:33,578 --> 00:11:36,592
Starting on an edge,
X wins fewer than half.

169
00:11:38,633 --> 00:11:41,710
So does going first mean X always wins?

170
00:11:42,030 --> 00:11:45,400
No. A computer can do more than count.

171
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:48,190
It starts at the bottom of the tree,

172
00:11:48,190 --> 00:11:51,413
where every game is finished,
and works upward.

173
00:11:51,733 --> 00:11:55,264
At every branch it picks the best move:

174
00:11:55,264 --> 00:12:00,062
best for X on X's turns,
and best for O on O's turns.

175
00:12:00,382 --> 00:12:02,987
Doing that, it has solved tic-tac-toe:

176
00:12:02,987 --> 00:12:06,962
if both players play perfectly,
every game ends in a draw.

177
00:12:07,282 --> 00:12:11,581
So every one of X's wins needs
a mistake by O somewhere.

178
00:12:14,016 --> 00:12:16,092
Could we do the same for chess?

179
00:12:16,412 --> 00:12:17,816
Not a chance.

180
00:12:17,866 --> 00:12:22,428
In 1950, the mathematician
and engineer Claude Shannon estimated

181
00:12:22,428 --> 00:12:26,847
that chess has at least 10 to the power
of 120 possible games.

182
00:12:27,167 --> 00:12:29,676
That's a 1 followed by 120 zeros!

183
00:12:29,996 --> 00:12:34,643
The whole observable universe has
only about 10 to the power of 80 atoms.

184
00:12:34,883 --> 00:12:38,691
So chess programs can't
explore every branch.

185
00:12:39,011 --> 00:12:42,817
They look only so many moves ahead,
then estimate who's winning,

186
00:12:42,817 --> 00:12:46,147
using what they learned
from millions of practice games.

187
00:12:46,467 --> 00:12:49,876
But underneath,
it's the same idea you just learned:

188
00:12:49,876 --> 00:12:52,956
a tree of moves,
explored one branch at a time.

189
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:54,917
Let's recap.

190
00:12:54,967 --> 00:13:01,458
Multiplying the choices gives
nine factorial, 362,880 orders.

191
00:13:01,778 --> 00:13:07,704
But games stop when someone wins,
so many of those orders are ghost games.

192
00:13:08,024 --> 00:13:13,958
And a short recursive program counts
every real game by trying a move,

193
00:13:13,958 --> 00:13:16,162
exploring, and undoing it.

194
00:13:16,482 --> 00:13:19,517
The answer is exactly 255,168.

195
00:13:19,766 --> 00:13:21,366
Now it's your turn.

196
00:13:21,566 --> 00:13:25,561
Change the program so it
counts only the games X wins,

197
00:13:25,561 --> 00:13:31,034
or only the games that end on move 7,
or see what happens if O goes first.

198
00:13:31,354 --> 00:13:34,592
The program and the challenges
are linked in the description.

199
00:13:34,912 --> 00:13:36,512
Have fun exploring!
